# issue-pilot configuration
# Copy to issue-pilot.conf next to this file (or point ISSUE_PILOT_CONF at it) and edit.
# Every value is sourced as shell — quote anything with spaces.

# --- Repo / queue ---
GH_REPO="owner/repo"                 # the repo whose issues are the work queue
READY_LABEL="status/approved"        # issues with this label are eligible for workers
CLAIM_LABEL="in-pilot"               # added when a lane picks an issue up

# --- Refill (issue generation) ---
REFILL_THRESHOLD=10                  # run the scanner when open ready issues drop below this
JANITOR_STALE_HOURS=6                # hourly janitor releases claims older than this with no PR
BLOCKED_LABEL="status/blocked"       # parked issues: workers can't finish them, a human must decide
PARK_AFTER_CLAIMS=4                  # janitor parks a ready issue claimed this often with no PR
JANITOR_WORKTREE_HOURS=48            # ...and deletes pilot/promote worktrees untouched this long
# Command that generates new issues (your scanner pipeline). Runs from ISSUE_PILOT_HOME.
# Issue quality is the foundation of the whole loop — use a strong model here.
SCANNER_CMD="echo 'no scanner configured' >&2; exit 1"
# Example: SCANNER_CMD='claude -p "$(cat examples/scanner.md)" --model opus --effort high --dangerously-skip-permissions'
# Per-dimension models: judgment-heavy scanners deserve stronger models than
# mechanical ones. SCANNER_CMD should use --model $SCANNER_RUN_MODEL --effort
# $SCANNER_RUN_EFFORT; refill resolves them per dimension (hyphens → underscores):
#SCANNER_MODEL_DEFAULT="opus"
#SCANNER_EFFORT_DEFAULT="high"
#SCANNER_MODEL_security="fable"      # costliest failures — strongest model
#SCANNER_MODEL_deps="sonnet"         # mostly mechanical — cheap model is enough
# Optional strict rotation: each refill runs the NEXT dimension in this list and
# exports it as $SCANNER_DIMENSION (use envsubst in SCANNER_CMD's prompt template):
#SCANNER_ROTATION="bugs security performance i18n product-gaps"
# Per-scanner cadence: minimum time between runs of a dimension ("3d", "12h", or
# seconds; unset = eligible every rotation turn). Rotation skips resting scanners;
# the panel's "Run next" bypasses the interval deliberately.
#SCANNER_INTERVAL_deps="7d"
#SCANNER_INTERVAL_security="3d"
#SCANNER_INTERVAL_test-gaps is written SCANNER_INTERVAL_test_gaps (hyphens → underscores)
# Location focus ring: code-reading dims rotate through these areas (comma-
# separated, entries may be prose) so every corner of the repo gets a deep read on
# a predictable cycle — without this, each stateless run resamples the same
# salient hotspots. Exported as $SCANNER_FOCUS to the prompt. Sensor dims
# (ci-health, prod-errors, ...) should NOT be listed in SCANNER_FOCUS_DIMS.
#SCANNER_FOCUS_DIMS="bugs security test-gaps product-gap performance"
#SCANNER_FOCUS_AREAS="src/api,src/web,packages/shared,scripts and tooling"
# Run ledger: refill exports $SCAN_LEDGER (recent tail) and $LEDGER_FILE to the
# prompt; the scanner template appends what each run examined / ruled out / left
# as leads, giving otherwise-stateless runs memory. No keys to set — automatic.

# Codex fallback: when every Claude account is at/ahead of its pace line, dims in
# SCANNER_FALLBACK_DIMS may scan on a Codex account instead of deferring. Keep the
# list to evidence-gated scanners — judgment-heavy dims should wait for Claude.
# Accounts: "name:/path/to/codex-home ..."; the least-used one below
# SCANNER_FALLBACK_MAX_USED (default 90) runs the scan.
#CODEX_ACCOUNTS="codex:$HOME/.codex work:$HOME/.codex-work"
#SCANNER_FALLBACK_DIMS="*"                # dims allowed to fall back ("*" = all, or a space list)
#SCANNER_FALLBACK_CMD='codex exec -m "$SCANNER_RUN_MODEL" -c model_reasoning_effort="$SCANNER_RUN_EFFORT" --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox "$(envsubst < examples/scanner.md)"'
#SCANNER_FALLBACK_MODEL="gpt-5.6-terra"
#SCANNER_FALLBACK_EFFORT="high"
# The prod-errors scanner needs a log source; it files nothing until this is set:
#ERROR_LOG_CMD='ssh prod-host "docker logs --since 24h api 2>&1 | grep -i error | tail -500"'
# Merge method for lanes and merge-janitor — must be one your repo allows
# (--merge, --squash, or --rebase). Default --merge.
#MERGE_METHOD="--merge"

# merge-janitor (runs hourly from refill, AUTO_MERGE=true only): merges open
# pilot PRs that are fully green and conflict-free — the ones whose batch died
# before CI concluded and that no worker would otherwise ever land.
# WIP limit: when open pilot PRs exceed this, pace throttles every lane to 1
# worker until the pile drains. Flow control, not a punishment.
#PR_WIP_LIMIT=25

# pr-doctor (runs hourly from refill): when >= PR_DOCTOR_MIN_SAME open pilot PRs
# fail the SAME check, files ONE base-breakage issue instead of letting every
# worker fight the shared failure alone. Zero LLM cost.
#PR_DOCTOR_PREFIX="pilot-"
#PR_DOCTOR_MIN_SAME=4
#PR_DOCTOR_LABELS="agent-created,status/approved,type/bug,priority/high"
#PR_DOCTOR_ASSIGNEE=""
# Label guard: after every scan, mechanically repair issues the agent under-labeled
# (type from the [Prefix] in the title, plus the labels/assignee below):
#SCANNER_GUARD=true
#SCANNER_GUARD_LABELS="agent-created"
#SCANNER_GUARD_PRIORITY="priority/medium"
#SCANNER_GUARD_ASSIGNEE=""
#SCANNER_TYPE_MAP="Perf=performance UI/UX=ui-ux"

# --- Batches ---
POLL_SECS=60                         # dispatcher poll interval
BASE_BRANCH="main"                   # branch subagents branch from and open PRs against
ISSUE_ORDER="oldest"                 # "oldest" or "newest" issues first
AUTO_MERGE=false                     # true = merge green-CI PRs; false = leave for review
BATCH_SIZE=25                        # issues per batch session (small batches survive session limits)
#REPO_DIR="/path/to/shared-clone"    # shared base clone; subagents branch worktrees off it (default: <issue-pilot>/repo)

# --- Lanes: the accounts that implement issues ---
# Each lane is one subscription running batch sessions. List ids in LANES, then set
# per-lane vars LANE_<id>_*:
#   LABEL         display name (shown in logs, status page, claim comments)
#   MODE          always = run whenever ready issues exist, fixed concurrency
#                 window = pace follower (work when behind the burn line, idle when not)
#                 off    = parked (config-level retire; day-to-day on/off is the
#                          panel's lane toggle, which pauses without touching MODE)
#   CMD           batch command; receives CONCURRENCY, BATCH_SIZE, GH_REPO, BASE_BRANCH,
#                 AUTO_MERGE, ISSUE_ORDER, SORT_DIR, READY_LABEL, CLAIM_LABEL, BLOCKED_LABEL, REPO_DIR,
#                 LANE_NAME, LANE_SLUG in its environment
#   CONCURRENCY   subagents per batch (always mode; window mode computes its own)
#   CREDENTIALS   (window) that account's .claude/.credentials.json for usage reading.
#                 NOTE: an account that runs no sessions gets a stale OAuth token and its
#                 usage becomes unreadable — schedule a tiny keepalive session, e.g. cron:
#                 0 */6 * * * CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<dir> claude -p "Reply OK" --dangerously-skip-permissions
#   USAGE_CMD     (window) override usage source; must print "<pct_used> <secs_to_reset>"
#                 (default: bin/usage-claude.sh with CREDENTIALS; Codex accounts:
#                 use "bash bin/usage-codex.sh")
#   MODEL/EFFORT  shown on the status page; keep in sync with the flags in CMD
#                 (claude: --model X --effort Y · codex: -m X -c model_reasoning_effort=Y)
# Per-lane overrides of the window defaults below: LANE_<id>_PACE_TOLERANCE_PCT,
# LANE_<id>_CATCHUP_HOURS, LANE_<id>_MIN_CONCURRENCY, LANE_<id>_MAX_CONCURRENCY,
# LANE_<id>_BURN_PCT_PER_WORKER_HOUR.
LANES=""
# Example — Codex grinding full-time, a Claude sub kept on its pace line:
# LANES="codex main"
# LANE_codex_LABEL="Codex"
# LANE_codex_MODE="always"
# LANE_codex_CONCURRENCY=3
# LANE_codex_CMD='codex exec --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox "$(envsubst < examples/goal.md)"'
#   (headless batches need the sandbox bypassed, same as claude's --dangerously-skip-permissions;
#    note codex has no subagents — it works through the batch sequentially)
# LANE_main_LABEL="Claude main"
# LANE_main_MODE="window"
# LANE_main_CREDENTIALS="$HOME/.claude/.credentials.json"
# LANE_main_CMD='claude -p "$(envsubst < examples/goal.md)" --dangerously-skip-permissions'

# --- Dynamic concurrency: server resource budget ---
# Each pace tick computes how many workers the box can afford — min of
# (cores − load5) / CORES_PER_WORKER and mem_available / MEM_MB_PER_WORKER — and
# caps every lane's concurrency by what's left of that budget. The budget is
# allocated in LANES order: put deadline-critical window lanes FIRST so an
# overloaded box starves the always-on workhorse before the expiring quota.
DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY=3                # always-mode lanes without their own CONCURRENCY
CORES_PER_WORKER=2                   # cores one worker (agent + builds/tests) roughly needs;
                                     # agents are mostly API-bound — use 1 on a busy shared box
MEM_MB_PER_WORKER=3000               # memory one worker roughly needs
RESOURCE_MIN_BUDGET=3                # never squeeze the total below this ("the default is 3")
#RESOURCE_PROBE_CMD='echo "16 2.5 48000"'  # override "<cores> <load5> <mem_avail_mb>" (tests, non-Linux)

# --- Window-mode defaults (pace follower) ---
# The ideal line runs 0% right after the account's reset to 100% at the next one.
# A window lane works whenever usage falls behind that line, and idles once it's
# back on pace — all week long, not just before the reset.
PACE_TOLERANCE_PCT=5                 # how far behind the line counts as "behind"
                                     # (also your personal headroom: raise it to keep
                                     # more slack for interactive use)
CATCHUP_HOURS=4                      # close a deficit within roughly this many hours
MIN_CONCURRENCY=1                    # floor while a window lane is catching up
HARD_STOP_PCT=98                     # even always-lanes stop when their account hits this —
                                     # grinding a spent account against rate limits helps nobody
MAX_CONCURRENCY=6                    # ceiling per lane
BURN_PCT_PER_WORKER_HOUR=2           # rough % of weekly quota one subagent burns per hour;
                                     # the pacer re-reads real usage each tick, so this
                                     # only needs to be in the right ballpark
FIVE_HOUR_THROTTLE_PCT=85            # when the account's 5-hour session window passes this…
FIVE_HOUR_THROTTLE_CAP=1             # …cap the lane here until it resets (Claude subscriptions
                                     # have BOTH a weekly and a 5-hour limit)

# --- Campaign (goal loop: state a goal, the system works toward it until done) ---
# `issue-pilot campaign set "<goal>"` starts one (one campaign at a time). Every
# tick, a gap-analysis agent compares reality against the goal, files issues for
# what's missing (labeled CAMPAIGN_LABEL — lanes take these first), and touches
# state/campaign/achieved when the goal verifiably holds. See examples/campaign.md.
CAMPAIGN_LABEL="campaign"
CAMPAIGN_MAX_ISSUES=10               # gaps filed per analysis round
CAMPAIGN_MIN_OPEN=3                  # re-analyze only when open campaign issues drop to this
#CAMPAIGN_BROWSER_URL=""             # e.g. your staging URL — enables real-browser validation
# Staging login for browser validation. Use a DEDICATED TEST ACCOUNT — this file is
# plaintext on the box, and agents screenshot logged-in pages into issues:
#CAMPAIGN_LOGIN_EMAIL=""
#CAMPAIGN_LOGIN_PASSWORD=""
CAMPAIGN_CMD='claude -p "$(envsubst < examples/campaign.md)" --model opus --effort high --dangerously-skip-permissions'

# --- Promote (release loop: BASE_BRANCH → STAGING_BRANCH → PROD_BRANCH) ---
# When enough commits have piled up on BASE_BRANCH, one strong agent session
# promotes them to production through PRs, FIXING CI along the way (expect CI to
# fail — that is most of the job). Merge commits only, one-way direction, frozen
# candidate. See examples/promote.md. Manual run: `issue-pilot promote --now`.
PROMOTE_ENABLED=false                # hourly timer acts only when true (and AUTO_MERGE=true)
PROMOTE_AFTER_COMMITS=100            # promote once this many commits wait BASE→STAGING
STAGING_BRANCH="staging"
PROD_BRANCH="main"
# Use your best model here — promotions are rare and high-stakes:
PROMOTE_CMD='claude -p "$(envsubst < examples/promote.md)" --model opus --effort max --dangerously-skip-permissions'
# Optional deterministic post-release check; non-zero exit marks the release
# "merged, runtime verification FAILED" instead of succeeded:
#PROMOTE_VERIFY_CMD='curl -sf https://api.example.com/healthz >/dev/null && curl -sfL https://example.com >/dev/null'
# Max seconds between promotion rounds waiting for the candidate CI to
# conclude (polled every 2 min; relaunches the moment checks finish):
#PROMOTE_ROUND_WAIT_MAX=2700
# --- Announce (optional): a release note for your NON-technical users ---
# After each verified release, announce.sh collects the feat/fix/perf commits that
# reached PROD_BRANCH since the last announcement, asks a model for a short
# plain-language note (your prompt, your language — see examples/announce.md,
# which sees $ANNOUNCE_COMMITS, $ANNOUNCE_CHANGELOG, $ANNOUNCE_COUNT,
# $ANNOUNCE_RANGE, $ANNOUNCE_VERSION = newest ANNOUNCE_TAG_PREFIX* tag on prod) and posts it with ANNOUNCE_POST_CMD ($MSG). A model reply of
# exactly SKIP posts nothing (internal-only release). Deferred/failed runs retry on
# the hourly promote tick; the panel's Release card has Preview / Post buttons.
ANNOUNCE_ENABLED=false
ANNOUNCE_CMD='claude -p "$(envsubst < examples/announce.md)" --model sonnet --dangerously-skip-permissions'
# Codex fallback when no Claude account is behind its pace line (writes to $ANNOUNCE_OUT
# because codex exec's stdout is noisy):
#ANNOUNCE_FALLBACK_CMD='codex exec -m gpt-5.6-terra -s read-only -o "$ANNOUNCE_OUT" "$(envsubst < examples/announce.md)" >/dev/null'
# Telegram example (forum topic): chat id of the group, message_thread_id of the topic
#ANNOUNCE_POST_CMD='curl -sS --fail-with-body -X POST "https://api.telegram.org/bot$ANNOUNCE_TG_TOKEN/sendMessage" --data-urlencode "chat_id=$ANNOUNCE_TG_CHAT" --data-urlencode "message_thread_id=$ANNOUNCE_TG_THREAD" --data-urlencode "text=$MSG" --data-urlencode "disable_web_page_preview=true" | jq -e .ok >/dev/null'
#ANNOUNCE_TG_TOKEN="123456:ABC..."   # bot must be a member of the group
#ANNOUNCE_TG_CHAT="-1001234567890"
#ANNOUNCE_TG_THREAD="2"
#ANNOUNCE_CHANGELOG_FILE=CHANGELOG.md # optional: feed the lines added in the range too
#ANNOUNCE_TAG_PREFIX=v                # release tags look like v1.2.3
#ANNOUNCE_COMMIT_FILTER='^(feat|fix|perf)(\(|:|!)'  # grep -E over commit subjects
# Tip: reserve CI runners for promotion PRs (route jobs whose base is
# STAGING_BRANCH/PROD_BRANCH, or promote/* heads, to a dedicated runner label) so
# releases never queue behind the worker lanes' CI.

# --- Claude account routing for orchestration roles ---
# Scanner, campaign, and promotion sessions are the heavyweight ones. List your
# Claude accounts ("name:config-dir") and each session bills to whichever account
# is furthest BEHIND its pace line at launch. Accounts at or ahead of their line
# are never used (the session defers and retries) — so no account is exhausted
# before its reset. Leave empty to use the default account.
#CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS="main:$HOME/.claude work:$HOME/.claude-work"
#ORCH_MAX_DRIFT=0                    # eligible only when drift is below this (0 = must be behind the line)
#ORCH_MAX_USED=95                    # never touch an account above this usage, regardless of drift

# --- Notifications ---
# Runs whenever a lane's concurrency changes. Gets $MSG.
NOTIFY_CMD=""
# Example: NOTIFY_CMD='curl -s -d "$MSG" ntfy.sh/my-topic'

# --- Status page (bin/status.sh + issue-pilot-web.service) ---
# Accounts shown on the page: semicolon-separated "name|kind|path" entries.
#   kind=claude → path is that account's .credentials.json
#   kind=codex  → path is the ~/.codex/sessions directory
#STATUS_ACCOUNTS="Claude main|claude|$HOME/.claude/.credentials.json;Codex|codex|$HOME/.codex/sessions"
